                                <item>
                                    <note xml:lang="en" xml:id="harkavy-cards">
                                        <p>Avraham E. Harkavy (1835-1919) was appointed to the department of Jewish literature and oriental manuscripts at the Imperial Public Library of St. Petersburg in 1868. In 1876, he was made head of the Department of Hebrew Books (Hebrew Department), and in 1877 appointed as ordinary librarian, a position which he held for the rest of his life. He was the first curator of the Firkovich collections. His unpublished cataloging of these collections consists of slips of paper (“Harkavy-Cards”) placed together with each manuscript that record his identification and/or brief description of the respective manuscript. These slips were the foundation for his handwritten four-volume inventory of the Yevr.-Arab. section of the Second Firkovich Collection.</p>
                                    </note>
                                    <persName>Avraham Harkavy</persName>
                                    <quote xml:lang="ru">
                                        <p>p. 6<lb/>N. 4508 (169)<lb/>Изъ араб. перев. и коммент. Кн. Левитъ<lb/>Давида б. Боазъ<lb/>V<lb/>… число листовъ оказалось не 337, а триста тридцать восемь (338)<lb/>[подпись]<lb/>338 л.<lb/>N. 4508 (169)<lb/>337л. изъ араб. перев. и коммент. Кн. Левитъ<lb/>Давида б. Боазъ<lb/>ч. поврежд., н ѣкот. л. дополнены поздн. рукою, припис. влад.<lb/>… число листовъ оказалось не 337, а триста тридцать восемь (338)<lb/>[подпись]<lb/>IV<lb/>corresponds to p. 2</p>
                                    </quote>
                                    <bibl>
                                        <title>Avraham E. Harkavy. Harkavy Cards (ru.)</title>
                                        <ptr target="https://jalit.org/bibl/BTVBSSXB"/>
                                    </bibl>
                                </item>
                                <item>
                                    <note xml:lang="en" xml:id="harkavy-cards">
                                        <p>Avraham E. Harkavy (1835-1919) was appointed to the department of Jewish literature and oriental manuscripts at the Imperial Public Library of St. Petersburg in 1868. In 1876, he was made head of the Department of Hebrew Books (Hebrew Department), and in 1877 appointed as ordinary librarian, a position which he held for the rest of his life. He was the first curator of the Firkovich collections. His unpublished cataloging of these collections consists of slips of paper (“Harkavy-Cards”) placed together with each manuscript that record his identification and/or brief description of the respective manuscript. These slips were the foundation for his handwritten four-volume inventory of the Yevr.-Arab. section of the Second Firkovich Collection.</p>
                                    </note>
                                    <persName>Avraham Harkavy</persName>
                                    <quote xml:lang="en">
                                        <p>p. 6<lb/>N. 4508 (169)<lb/>From the Arabic translation and commentary on the Book of Leviticus<lb/>By David ben Boaz<lb/>V<lb/>… the number of folios turned out to be not 337, but three hundred thirty-eight (338).<lb/>[Signature]<lb/>338 folios.<lb/>N. 4508 (169)<lb/>337 folios from the Arabic translation and commentary on the Book of Leviticus<lb/>By David ben Boaz<lb/>Partly damaged, some folios have been added by a later hand, additional note by the owner<lb/>… the number of folios turned out to be not 337, but three hundred thirty-eight (338).<lb/>[Signature]<lb/>IV</p>
                                    </quote>
                                    <bibl>
                                        <title>Avraham E. Harkavy. Harkavy Cards (en.)</title>
                                        <ptr target="https://jalit.org/bibl/BTVBSSXB"/>
                                    </bibl>
                                </item>
